  note={
    The original published description of Io, a small prototype-based
    object-oriented language created by Dekorte starting in 2002.
    Io combines ideas from Smalltalk, Self, NewtonScript, Act1, Lisp and Lua.
    Its objects contain slots and a list of prototypes; lookup recursively
    searches these prototypes, and new objects are made by differential
    inheritance through cloning.
    The language also provides actors and futures for concurrency, coroutines,
    runtime-accessible code and a deliberately minimal message-based syntax.
    Multiple inheritance uses a simple depth-first lookup of messages through the ancestry
    (which can explode exponentially with diamonds).
  }
}

